WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.300
Safe methods: a safety plan, crisp chips, and clean drying.

2
00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:08.830
This is the video for Module 2 of Small-Scale Food Processing.

3
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:19.700
The World Health Organization gives five keys to safer food. First, keep clean. Wash hands and tools, and keep insects, rats and pets out.

4
00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:25.280
Second, separate raw and cooked. Use separate bowls, trays and boards.

5
00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:32.330
Third, cook thoroughly. Roast and fry until fully done inside, not just browned outside.

6
00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:43.840
Fourth, keep food at safe temperatures. Between 5 C and 60 C, bacteria multiply fast. Do not leave moist cooked food out for more than 2 hours.

7
00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:49.360
Fifth, use safe water, and reject mouldy or damaged raw food.

8
00:00:50.610 --> 00:01:05.010
HACCP means Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points. A hazard is anything that can make food unsafe: biological, like mould; chemical, like aflatoxin or cyanide; or physical, like stones or hair.

9
00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:11.400
Next, pick the critical control points. These are the steps where control is essential.

10
00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:22.490
At each one, set a critical limit you can check with your eyes, hands or a thermometer. For chips, oil at 160 C to 180 C.

11
00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:27.640
Decide how often you check it, and what you do if the limit is missed.

12
00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:32.620
And write it down every batch, on one page you stick on the wall.

13
00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:48.170
For example, Halima in Kaduna makes groundnut paste. Her main danger is aflatoxin, a poison made by moulds. Roasting does not destroy it reliably, and long-term exposure is linked to liver cancer.

14
00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:59.380
So her critical control point is sorting. Her limit: no mouldy, shrivelled or discoloured nuts, and no stones. If mould is widespread, the whole lot is rejected.

15
00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:05.510
At roasting, she cracks a few nuts open to check they are dry and crunchy throughout.

16
00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:13.290
At grinding, she checks the shared mill is clean, and runs a small handful through first, then throws it away.

17
00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:23.310
Now a full chips batch. Use firm, fully green plantain. Wash before peeling, and oil your hands or wear gloves against the sap.

18
00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:33.090
Slice evenly, about 1 to 2 mm. A hand slicer works far better than a knife. Thin slices burn while thick ones stay soft.

19
00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:43.940
Heat the oil to 160 C to 180 C, checked with a thermometer. Too cool and chips soak up oil. Too hot and they burn outside.

20
00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:49.760
Fry in small batches. Overloading the pot drops the oil temperature.

21
00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:57.450
Drain on a rack and cool completely under a clean net. Warm chips in a pouch release steam and go soft.

22
00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.670
Seal with an impulse sealer and label with the date and batch number.

23
00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:08.780
Keep water away from hot oil, and change oil when it turns dark, smells sharp or smokes.

24
00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:19.390
Write down weights at each stage, so you plan from real numbers. For example, Chiamaka in Onitsha weighs 10 fingers at 3.0 kg.

25
00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.030
After peeling there is about 1.9 kg of flesh.

26
00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:31.600
After frying, about 0.85 kg of chips remain, because most of the water is gone.

27
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:40.640
That fills 21 pouches of 40 g. Now she can plan purchases and costing from real numbers instead of guesses.

28
00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:49.780
Drying on bare ground lets in dust, stones, droppings and insects, and slow drying in humid weather invites mould.

29
00:03:51.030 --> 00:04:00.140
Use a raised rack covered with net, or a solar dryer. Spread food in one thin layer, turn it daily, and cover it at night and before rain.

30
00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:09.260
Cool first, then test. Pieces should snap. Then seal a handful in a dry jar for a day. Droplets mean more drying.

31
00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:22.220
For example, Grace's 5 kg of fresh pepper dried down to about 0.6 kg. Pack once it is cool, store it off the floor, and never add insecticides to stored food.

32
00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:39.620
To remember. Follow the five keys. Give every critical point a limit, a check, a fix and a record. Control aflatoxin by sorting and dry storage. Fry at the right temperature and cool before sealing. And dry food off the ground, then test it.

33
00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:49.850
Now write your one-page safety plan, fry a small test batch and record its weights, and dry one product, then check it weekly for four weeks.

34
00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.140
Continue with the lessons below.
